Short:        minigl.library for R200 Radeons on P96
Uploader:     marcus.gerards@googlemail.com (Marcus Gerards)
Author:       marcus.gerards@googlemail.com (Marcus Gerards)
Type:         driver/video
Version:      @VERSION@
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 3.1
Distribution: Aminet
Replaces:     driver/video/RadeonGL.lha
Requires:     A Radeon 8500, 9000, 9100, 9200 or 9250 driven by
              Picasso96 3.6+ (Radeon.card); a 68040 or 68060 with FPU,
              or a 68020/68030 with 68881/68882 (a second build);
              AmigaOS 3.1+ (tested on 3.2).
Kurz:         minigl.library fuer R200-Radeons an P96

RadeonGL @VERSION@  --  @DATE@


WHAT IT IS

A minigl.library that draws with the 3D engine of an ATI Radeon from the
R200 family (8500, 9000, 9100, 9200, 9250) on a graphics card that
Picasso96 drives. No Warp3D: it talks to the Radeon's command processor
through Picasso96, while the Workbench keeps running.

It is a minigl.library 29.1 with dispatch ABI 5, so programs built with
the current MiniGL SDK open it like any other minigl.library 29.


SOURCE CODE

  https://github.com/bubbob42/RadeonGL

The archive carries it too, in the drawer Source (no icon: Show All
Files, or a Shell): the library, the Installer, the tools, the demos, the
tests and the makefiles. Building needs m68k-amigaos-gcc, the Picasso96
developer files and, for the probes, Thomas Richter's openpci68k.lha
(Aminet driver/other); Source/README.md explains it.


FEATURES

  * Every GL primitive, in immediate mode and from vertex arrays (8-, 16-
    and 32-bit indices)
  * Compiled vertex arrays as indexed draws; an upload cache keeps what a
    program sends again unchanged in the card's memory
  * Transformation and rasterising on the Radeon, the full float matrix
    stack
  * Two texture units; RGB565, ARGB4444, I8, AI88 in up to 16 MB of card
    memory; nearest, linear and mipmap filters; repeat and clamp;
    MODULATE, REPLACE, DECAL (RGB), ADD
  * Blending, alpha test, depth test, polygon offset, culling, scissor,
    colour mask, flat and smooth shading, point size
  * A Picasso96 screen of its own, 16 or 32 bit, up to 2048 x 2048
  * Every command list checked before the Radeon runs it (can be turned
    off for speed); one Picasso96 lock a frame
  * Installer, manuals in English and German, a card test, a card report
    for bug reports, four demos


NOT IMPLEMENTED YET

100 of minigl.library 29.1's 205 functions are there: all GLQuake uses,
and more. A missing one sets GL_INVALID_OPERATION; the log names it.

  * Lighting and materials, fog, display lists, texture coordinate
    generation
  * GL_BLEND and RGBA GL_DECAL texture environments; 32-bit textures are
    stored with 16 bits
  * Stencil and accumulation buffers, stipple, logic operations
  * glReadPixels, glCopyTexImage2D, glPixelStorei, glDrawBuffer
  * Double-precision calls, glArrayElement, interleaved arrays, colour
    index, glBlendEquation, glLineWidth, glColorTable
  * GLU and GLUT; MiniGL's own main loop; contexts in a window or bitmap
  * One program at a time; textures beyond 16 MB are not swapped out


GLQUAKE BENCHMARKS

GLQuake for R200 68k (game/shoot/GLQuakeR200, the RadeonGL edition of
GLQuake for minigl.library, 1.0), its timedemos on the test machine
(A4000D, 68060 at 50 MHz on a CyberStorm MK II, Mediator, Radeon 9200,
AmigaOS 3.2, Picasso96 3.6), RadeonGL 0.7, command check off
(RadeonGLCheck=0), frames a second:

    Demo   320 x 240   640 x 480   1280 x 720
    demo1       29.4        28.7         28.0
    demo2       25.1           -         21.1
    demo3       25.6           -         24.0

The Aminet source built for the 68060, on RadeonGL 0.6: demo1 24.4 /
23.7 / 23.3, demo2 21.7 / - / 17.9, demo3 21.4 / - / 19.9. With the
command check on (as installed) slower: 0.4 took demo1 from 24.4 to
19.1. The 68060 sets the pace, not the Radeon.
A tester's A4000D (BFG9060, 68060 at 75 MHz, Prometheus Firestorm PCI
bridge, RadeonGL 0.6, check off, demo1 at 320 x 240 without sound):
43.0 fps. RadeonGL works with both the Mediator and the Prometheus.


INSTALLATION

Double-click Install. It keeps a minigl.library that is not RadeonGL's
as LIBS:minigl.library.old. RadeonGL.guide explains going back.


MADE WITH AI

RadeonGL was made with the help of AI. Claude, an AI model by Anthropic,
wrote most of its code, its tests and its manuals, working under the
direction of Marcus Gerards, who made the design decisions. Every release
was tested on real hardware.


